---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site.js";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Services | Oliver Hitchings"
  description="Automation mapping, focused builds, and handover packages for repeatable business tasks."
>
  <main class="subpage services-page">
    <section class="services-hero">
      <div class="container services-hero__grid">
        <div class="services-hero__copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Services</p>
          <h1>Start with one repeat task and the right-sized first step.</h1>
          <p class="services-hero__intro">
            Choose a mapping sprint, a focused build or a wider operator system. Each option has
            visible scope, pricing and handover.
          </p>
          <div class="cta-row left">
            <a class="button" href="#packages">Compare packages</a>
            <a class="button ghost" href="#contact">Start an enquiry</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <aside class="services-hero__trace" aria-label="Service sequence">
          <span>Working sequence</span>
          <p class="method-trace">Map → Build → Hand over</p>
          <p>One bounded path, with ownership and review made explicit before it grows.</p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container editorial-section services-method" data-services-method data-reveal>
      <div class="editorial-heading compact-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
          <h2>Map the work before building the loop.</h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="services-editorial-rows">
        <article data-method-step>
          <span>01</span>
          <h3>Map</h3>
          <p>Name the trigger, inputs, owner, approvals and failure states.</p>
        </article>
        <article data-method-step>
          <span>02</span>
          <h3>Build</h3>
          <p>Create a narrow loop with review, logs and clear stop conditions.</p>
        </article>
        <article data-method-step>
          <span>03</span>
          <h3>Hand over</h3>
          <p>Leave the prompts, logs, run instructions and change notes with the owner.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="packages" class="container editorial-section services-packages" data-reveal>
      <div class="editorial-heading compact-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Prices and packages</p>
          <h2>Choose the smallest useful first step.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="editorial-intro">
          The scope expands only when the task needs it. Every option names the work and the
          handover before a build begins.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="services-package-rows">
        <article class="services-package-row">
          <header>
            <p class="eyebrow">Fixed scope</p>
            <h3>Task Map</h3>
            <p class="price">£250</p>
          </header>
          <div>
            <p>
              Best when you know something should be automated, but the exact build shape is still
              unclear. This turns one repeat task into a scoped plan.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>One discovery session</li>
              <li>Current process, owner, inputs, and output map</li>
              <li>Failure points, approval moments, and edge cases</li>
              <li>Recommended build shape, tools, and next-step estimate</li>
            </ul>
            <a class="button ghost" href="#contact">Enquire</a>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="services-package-row">
          <header>
            <p class="eyebrow">Focused implementation</p>
            <h3>First Build</h3>
            <p class="price">£500</p>
          </header>
          <div>
            <p>
              A focused first implementation for one use case. The aim is to prove the task can run
              with useful controls before it becomes part of the business.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>Task map and implementation plan</li>
              <li>Working automation using real inputs and outputs</li>
              <li>Prompts, logs, review rules, and failure handling</li>
              <li>Run guide plus two review sessions after first use</li>
            </ul>
            <a class="button" href="#contact">Start here</a>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="services-package-row">
          <header>
            <p class="eyebrow">Connected workflow</p>
            <h3>Operator System</h3>
            <p class="price">£1,000</p>
          </header>
          <div>
            <p>
              For work that has more than one step, owner, or system involved. This creates a more
              complete operating setup around the automation.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>Multiple task paths or departments</li>
              <li>Monitoring, alerts, reporting, and exception handling</li>
              <li>Documentation, owner training, and change notes</li>
              <li>30-day improvement period after launch</li>
            </ul>
            <a class="button ghost" href="#contact">Discuss scope</a>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="services-package-row services-package-row--support">
          <header>
            <p class="eyebrow">Optional retainer</p>
            <p class="price">from £100/month</p>
          </header>
          <div>
            <p>
              For teams that want a light monthly support layer after launch: monitoring, small
              improvements, fixes, usage checks, and one review call.
            </p>
            <a class="button ghost" href="#contact">Add support</a>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="package-note">
        Guide prices. VAT, third-party software, model usage and hosting are separate where they
        apply.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container editorial-section good-fit-section" data-good-fit>
      <div class="editorial-heading compact-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Good-fit criteria</p>
          <h2>Frequent enough to matter. Clear enough to inspect.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="editorial-intro">
          A useful first candidate usually has a named trigger, repeat inputs, an expected output
          and a person who can review it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="criteria-rows">
        <article>
          <h3>Frequent</h3>
          <p>The task happens often enough for repeatability to matter.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Structured</h3>
          <p>The trigger, inputs and expected output can be named.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Reviewable</h3>
          <p>A person can inspect the result and approve what happens next.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Contained</h3>
          <p>The first useful boundary is small enough to map and test.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container editorial-section handover-section" data-handover data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow">Handover</p>
      <div class="handover-section__grid">
        <h2>What stays with the owner.</h2>
        <div>
          <p>
            First Build and Operator System include prompts, run instructions, logs, review rules,
            failure states and owner notes—the material needed to operate, inspect and change the
            system.
          </p>
          <p>
            Task Map ends with the process map and build recommendation, so the next decision is
            clear before implementation starts.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="contact" class="container editorial-section contact-panel enquiry-section">
      <div class="enquiry-section__grid">
        <div class="enquiry-section__heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Start the conversation</p>
          <h2>Describe the repeat task.</h2>
          <p class="contact-intro">
            Tell me what happens now, which tools are involved, who checks the output and what a
            useful result would look like. This form sends the enquiry directly from the website.
          </p>
        </div>

        <form
          class="contact-form"
          action="/api/contact"
          method="POST"
          aria-describedby="contact-form-status"
          data-contact-form
        >
          <input type="text" name="_honey" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" aria-hidden="true" />

          <div class="form-grid">
            <label>
              <span>Name</span>
              <input name="name" type="text" autocomplete="name" required />
            </label>
            <label>
              <span>Email</span>
              <input name="email" type="email" autocomplete="email" required />
            </label>
            <label>
              <span>Contact number (optional)</span>
              <input name="contact_number" type="tel" autocomplete="tel" />
            </label>
            <label>
              <span>Package interest</span>
              <select name="package_interest" required>
                <option value="">Choose one</option>
                <option>Task Map</option>
                <option>First Build</option>
                <option>Operator System</option>
                <option>Ongoing support</option>
                <option>Not sure yet</option>
              </select>
            </label>
          </div>

          <label>
            <span>What do you want automated?</span>
            <textarea
              name="automation_request"
              rows="5"
              required
              placeholder="Tell me the repeat task, who handles it now, and what a good result would look like."
            ></textarea>
          </label>

          <label>
            <span>Tools or systems involved</span>
            <textarea
              name="tools_involved"
              rows="3"
              placeholder="For example: Gmail, Outlook, Sheets, Notion, Slack, CRM, website forms, internal docs."
            ></textarea>
          </label>

          <button class="button" type="submit">Send enquiry</button>
          <p
            id="contact-form-status"
            class="form-status"
            role="status"
            aria-live="polite"
            aria-atomic="true"
            data-contact-status
          >
            This sends the enquiry directly from the website to {site.email}.
          </p>
        </form>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <script>
    import { initContactForms } from "../scripts/contact-form.js";

    initContactForms();
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
